/// Chapter 9 of the guide, written from md-docs/09-events.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page EventsGuide(path: "/docs/guide/events", title: "Events", description: "DOM event handlers, the event object, keyboard shortcuts, component events, modals, menus, popovers, gestures and window events.", layout: DocsShell("Events", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "9" }), blurb: "Handlers on elements, keyboard shortcuts, the events components fire, the overlays a page opens, and drag and drop.", source: "md-docs/09-events.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "handlers", label: "Handlers"), Anchor(id: "keys", label: "Keys"), Anchor(id: "emitting-events-from-components", label: "Emitting events from components"), Anchor(id: "overlays-modals-dialogs-toasts-and-menus", label: "Overlays: modals, dialogs, toasts and menus"), Anchor(id: "gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter", label: "Gestures: drag, long-press, swipe and the splitter"), Anchor(id: "listening-outside-the-page-s-elements", label: "Listening outside the page's elements"), Anchor(id: "summary", label: "Summary")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/state", prevLabel: "8. {t("ch.08")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/control-flow", nextLabel: "10. {t("ch.10")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("An event handler is the code that runs when something happens: a click, a key, a drop, a component saying it changed. You write it as `on <event> \{ … \}` inside the block of the element it listens to, and its body can change state, call actions and navigate.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- handlers and the event object\n- keys, and shortcuts for a whole app\n- the events your own components fire\n- overlays: modals, toasts, menus, popovers and tooltips, and how they stack\n- gestures: drag and drop, long-press, swipe and the splitter\n- listening to the window and the document")

        Heading("Handlers", id: "handlers").h2

        Markdown("A handler is written inside an element's block: `on <event> \{ … \}`, or `on <event>(name) \{ … \}` when the body reads the event object. This page counts clicks and logs what happens in a text field:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Clicks(path: "/", title: "Clicks", description: "Handlers on a button and a field.") {
    state n = 0
    state last = ""
    Heading("Clicks").h1
    Button("+1") { on click { n = n + 1 } }
    Input(bind: last, placeholder: "Type here") {
        on input { log(last) }
        on keydown(e) { if e.key == "Enter" { log("submitted {last}") } }
        on focus { log("in") }
        on blur { log("out") }
    }
    Text("{n}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A handler may name any DOM event. The common ones are `click`, `input`, `change`, `submit`, `focus`, `blur`, `keydown`, `keyup`, `mouseenter` and `mouseleave`. The rest — `dblclick`, `contextmenu`, `pointerdown`, `pointermove`, `touchstart`, `wheel`, `scroll`, `paste`, `drop`, `dragover`, `animationend`, `ended` and the others — work the same way. The [components reference](/docs/reference#on-every-element) lists every one. On a call of your own component, the events it declares ([Components](/docs/guide/components#events)) join them.\n\nA handler's body is an imperative block, so it may assign state, call actions, `await` and `navigate`.\n\nA handler on a component call — `UserCard(\"Sam\") \{ on click \{ … \} \}` — attaches to the component's root element, so a styled button component is clickable wherever it is used.")

        Heading("The event object", id: "the-event-object").h3

        Markdown("To read the event, name a parameter: `on keydown(e) \{ … \}`. It is the browser's own event object, so `e.key`, `e.target.value`, `e.clientX`, `e.preventDefault()` and `e.stopPropagation()` are all there.\n\nWithout a parameter, the event is still in scope as `event` — unless the program has an `event` of its own (a loop's item, a state, an action), which wins. Name the parameter to read the event there.\n\nA DOM event sends one value, the event, so a handler names one name at most: `on click(e, x)` is an error (`T22`). Read what you want from the event, `e.clientX`. Several names are for a component's own events, which send what they declare ([below](#emitting-events-from-components)).\n\nThis drop zone uses the event object twice — to allow the drop, and to read the dropped files:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Drop(path: "/", title: "Drop", description: "A place to drop files.") {
    state names: [String] = []
    Heading("Drop files here").h1
    Card {
        on dragover(e) { e.preventDefault() }
        on drop(e) {
            e.preventDefault()
            names = Array.from(e.dataTransfer.files).map(f => f.name)
        }
        Text(if names.length == 0 { "Nothing yet" } else { names.join(", ") })
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Keys", id: "keys").h2

        Markdown("`on key(\"…\")` answers to one key, with modifiers. On an element it fires while that element has focus; at the top of a page or component it fires anywhere on the page:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Keys(path: "/", title: "Keys", description: "Keys on a field and on the page.") {
    state open = false
    state q = ""
    Heading("Keys").h1
    Input(bind: q, ref: box, placeholder: "Search") {
        on key("Escape") { q = "" }            // on the element: while it has focus
        on key("Enter", e) { log(e.target.value) }
    }
    on key("ctrl+k") { box.focus() }           // on the page: anywhere on the page
    on key("cmd+k") { box.focus() }
    on key("?") { open = !open }
    if open { Alert("Shortcuts: ctrl+k to search, ? for this.").info }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How a combination is spelled:\n\n- Modifiers are `ctrl`, `shift`, `alt` and `meta` (or `cmd`), joined with `+`.\n- Then comes the key as the browser names it: `k`, `Enter`, `Escape` (or `esc`), `Tab`, `ArrowDown` (or `down`), `space`, `F2`.\n- `on key(\"Enter\", e)` names the event too.\n\nHow it listens:\n\n- On an element, the handler listens on that element.\n- At the top of a page or component, it listens on the document for as long as the page shows.\n- Every modifier must match exactly, so `ctrl+k` does not fire on `ctrl+shift+k`.\n\nA combination no keyboard sends — an unknown modifier, no key — is an error (`E117`).")

        Heading("Shortcuts for an app", id: "shortcuts-for-an-app").h3

        Markdown("To give an app shortcuts that work on every page, write them with `mod` and declare them on `app`:\n\n- **`mod`** is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl everywhere else.\n- On **`app`**, a shortcut is heard on every page for as long as the app runs.\n- A **chord** is its steps with a space between: `mod+k mod+s`. The second step must come within a second and a half of the first, and on its own it does not fire a shortcut of its own.\n\nThis app drives its shortcuts and its **View** menu from one table of commands:")

        CodeBlock(#"store Commands {
    state said = ""
    state table: [Command] = [
        { id: "palette", keys: "mod+shift+p", title: "Commands" },
        { id: "sidebar", keys: "mod+b", title: "Toggle sidebar" },
    ]
    action run(e) {
        for c in table {
            if keyIs(e, c.keys) {
                e.preventDefault()
                said = c.id
            }
        }
    }
}

type Command { id: String, keys: String, title: String }

app {
    on key("mod+s") { Commands.said = "saved" }
    on key("mod+k mod+s") { Commands.said = "shortcut help" }
    on keydown(e) { Commands.run(e) }
    Menu(trigger: "View") {
        for c in Commands.table {
            Menu.Item(shortcut: c.keys) { Text(c.title) }
        }
    }
    Text("Commands: {keyLabel(\"mod+shift+p\")}")
    Router
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The helpers and rules it relies on:\n\n- **`keyIs(e, \"mod+s\")`** is whether a key event is that combination — a chord's too — so one table of commands can drive the shortcuts, the menus and a palette.\n- **`keyLabel(\"mod+shift+p\")`** is how this system writes it: `⇧⌘P` on a Mac, `Ctrl+Shift+P` elsewhere. A `Menu.Item`'s and a `Tooltip`'s `shortcut:` are written the same way, and the item — or the tooltip's control — tells assistive technology with `aria-keyshortcuts`.\n- **Events outside any element.** A page, a component or `app` may handle `keydown` and `keyup`, and the window's `resize`, `scroll`, `hashchange`, `popstate`, `online`, `offline`, `message`, `storage` and `visibilitychange`, and the window losing and gaining focus (`blur`, `focus` — an editor saves on leaving, for example). Each listens for as long as its owner shows.\n- **A letter is also the key in its place.** On a Mac `⌥⌘P` sends `π`, and `mod+alt+p` still matches it.")

        Heading("Emitting events from components", id: "emitting-events-from-components").h2

        Markdown("A component declares the events it fires with `event`, and fires them with `emit`. The caller handles them like DOM events, with a name for each value the event sends — `on change(v)`, `on menu(key, x, y)`. [Components](/docs/guide/components#events) covers the details. This counter tells its page every time it changes:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Counter(_ start: Number) {
    event change(value: Number)
    state n = start
    Row(gap: .sm) {
        Button("−") { on click { n = n - 1  emit change(n) } }
        Text("{n}")
        Button("+") { on click { n = n + 1  emit change(n) } }
    }
}

page Tally(path: "/", title: "Tally", description: "A component that reports its count.") {
    state total = 0
    Heading("Tally").h1
    Counter(10) { on change(v) { total = v } }
    Text("Now {total}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Overlays: modals, dialogs, toasts and menus", id: "overlays-modals-dialogs-toasts-and-menus").h2

        Markdown("An *overlay* is something a page opens over itself: a modal that asks a question, a toast that confirms, a menu of actions, a tooltip. Most are opened by a state, so the page decides when they show. This page has one of each:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Overlays(path: "/", title: "Overlays", description: "What a page opens.") {
    state confirm = false
    state deleted = false
    Heading("Overlays").h1
    Button("Delete").danger { on click { confirm = true } }
    Modal(visible: confirm, title: "Delete this?") {
        Text("This cannot be undone.")
        Modal.Footer {
            Button("Cancel") { on click { confirm = false } }
            Button("Delete").danger { on click { confirm = false  deleted = true } }
        }
    }
    if deleted { Toast("Deleted").success }
    Menu(trigger: "More") {
        Menu.Item { Text("Rename") }
        Menu.Divider
        Menu.Item { Text("Archive") }
    }
    Tooltip("Copies the link") { IconButton(icon: "copy", label: "Copy link") }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`Modal`** and **`Dialog`** are real `<dialog>` elements, open while their `visible:` state is true.\n  - The browser traps focus, makes the rest of the page inert and closes the dialog on Escape. A click on the backdrop leaves it open.\n  - When it closes that way, the state is set back to `false`, so it never disagrees with what is on screen. Focus returns to what opened it.\n  - `on close` runs when it closes, however it was closed.\n  - `title:` is its heading, which names it (`A08` without one). `Modal.Footer` is the row of actions.\n  - `Modal.Title \{ … \}` is what the heading draws when words alone will not do — `Modal.Title \{ Markdown(\"Rename `\{name\}`\") \}` — while `title:` stays its name.\n  - `.closable` puts a close button in the heading row, which closes it as Escape does.\n- **`Toast`** shows the moment it is rendered and leaves after three seconds. It is announced politely to screen readers. Put it under a condition that turns true.\n- **`Menu`** and **`Dropdown`** open a list from a button. Arrow keys move between items, Home and End jump to the ends, Enter and Space choose, Escape closes with focus back on the button, and a click outside closes it.\n- **`Tooltip`** shows its text when its child is hovered or focused, and is linked to it with `aria-describedby`.")

        Heading("Layers: popovers, menus and tooltips above everything", id: "layers-popovers-menus-and-tooltips-above-everything").h3

        Markdown("A `Popover`, a `Menu`'s list and a `Tooltip` are drawn in the browser's *top layer*, above everything else on the page. A box with `overflow: hidden`, or one with a stacking context of its own, cannot clip or cover them.\n\nEach layer is placed against its *anchor*, the element it belongs to:\n\n- It flips to the other side when there is no room, and slides along its side to stay on screen.\n- It follows the anchor while the page scrolls. When the anchor scrolls out of view the layer goes with it, still open, and comes back with it.\n\nThe open layers form one stack, newest on top. A press outside closes every layer it is outside of.\n\nEscape closes the newest layer that can be dismissed — a popover, a menu, an open `Modal` or `Dialog` — and stops there: nothing behind it hears that key. For example:\n\n- A modal opened from a popover is above it. The first Escape closes the modal, which its `on close` hears, and the next closes the popover.\n- A modal covers what is beneath it, so a press in it, or on its backdrop, leaves the popover under it open.\n- A tooltip keeps Escape only while focus is on its control: it hides, and the key goes no further. Otherwise the same Escape hides it on its way past and goes on, to the layer below or to the element with focus. So a tooltip under a pointer resting on a toolbar button does not take the Escape that closes an editor's completion list.\n\nThis editor has a popover at a button, a menu bar with every kind of item, a tooltip and a context menu at the pointer:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Editor(path: "/", title: "Editor", description: "Layers at a control and at the pointer.") {
    state branches = false
    state wrap = false
    state asked = false
    state point = { x: 0, y: 0 }
    state said = ""
    Heading("Editor").h1
    Row {
        Button("main", ref: branch) { on click { branches = !branches } }
        Menu(trigger: "View") {
            Menu.Item(shortcut: "Ctrl+B") {
                on click { said = "sidebar" }
                Text("Toggle sidebar")
            }
            Menu.Item(checked: wrap) {
                on click { wrap = !wrap }
                Text("Word wrap")
            }
            Menu.Item(disabled: true) { Text("Zen mode") }
            Menu.Divider
            Menu.Sub(label: "Appearance") {
                Menu.Item { Text("Light") }
                Menu.Item { Text("Dark") }
            }
        }
        Tooltip("Save", shortcut: "Ctrl+S", delay: 400) { Button("Save") }
    }
    Popover(visible: branches, anchor: branch, placement: .bottomStart, label: "Branches").arrow {
        on close(reason) { said = "closed by {reason}" }
        Text("main")
        Text("feature/layers")
    }
    Card {
        on contextmenu(e) {
            e.preventDefault()
            point = { x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY }
            asked = true
        }
        Text("Right-click for the context menu")
    }
    Menu(visible: asked, at: point) {
        Menu.Item(shortcut: "F12") { Text("Go to definition") }
        Menu.Item { Text("Rename") }
        Menu.Divider
        Menu.Item.danger { Text("Delete") }
    }
    Text(said)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("What each part does:\n\n- **`Popover`** is open while its `visible:` state is true. It is placed against `anchor:` — an element's `ref:` — on `placement:` (`.bottom`, `.bottomStart`, `.bottomEnd`, `.top…`, `.left…`, `.right…`; unset, `.bottomStart`), `offset:` pixels away. When Escape or a press outside closes it, `visible:` is set back to `false` and `on close(reason)` hears why: `\"escape\"` or `\"outside\"`. `.arrow` draws a point toward the anchor; `label:` is its accessible name.\n- **`Menu`** with `trigger:` is a button and its list. Without one it is a list that state opens — open from the first paint if its state starts `true` — at an `anchor:`, or `at:` a point `\{ x, y \}`, where the pointer asked for a context menu. `label:` names the list for a screen reader; it is never a button's text. Choosing an item closes the menu, and `on close` hears `\"choose\"`.\n- **`Menu.Item`** takes:\n  - `shortcut:` — the keys that do the same, drawn at its end and given to assistive technology as `aria-keyshortcuts`;\n  - `checked:` — makes it a check item that follows its state;\n  - `disabled:` — keeps it in sight but out of reach: the arrow keys skip it and a press does nothing;\n  - `.danger` — for what cannot be taken back (Delete, Discard), drawn in the danger colour.\n- **`Menu.Sub(label:)`** is an entry with a menu of its own, opened beside it by a hover, ArrowRight or Enter. ArrowLeft and Escape go back to the entry. Choosing in it closes the whole menu, and it closes with its menu.\n- **`Tooltip`** waits `delay:` milliseconds of hover (unset, 500) and shows at once for keyboard focus. It never takes focus, and goes on leaving, on a press and on Escape. Once Escape has hidden it, it stays hidden until the pointer comes back to it or keyboard focus reaches its control. `shortcut:` adds a chip with the keys and gives the control it names its `aria-keyshortcuts`, in ARIA's spelling (`Control+S`). `placement:` is where it goes (unset, `.top`).\n\nA layer takes what any element takes ([Elements](/docs/guide/elements)), on the element it draws: `id:`, `role:`, `tabindex:`, `title:`, `aria-*`, `data-*`, `ref:`, and its `on` handlers. Where they land:\n\n- A `Popover` is a `dialog` unless its `role:` says what else it is — a `listbox` of choices, for example.\n- A `Menu` with a `trigger:` takes them on the box around its button and list. One that state opens takes them on its list, whose `role:` then replaces `menu`.\n- A `Tooltip` takes them on the box around its control.\n- A `Modal` or a `Dialog` takes them on its `<dialog>`, whose `title:` is its heading rather than an attribute.\n\nA `Popover`'s or a `Menu`'s `on close` is the layer's own, and hears why it closed.")

        Heading("Gestures: drag, long-press, swipe and the splitter", id: "gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter").h2

        Markdown("The runtime turns what the pointer does over time into one event that says what it means. A mouse, a pen and a finger run the same code. A finger picks a drag up with a long press, so a plain swipe still scrolls the page.\n\nThis file browser reorders tabs, moves tree rows into a folder, and sizes its sidebar with a splitter:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Files(path: "/", title: "Files", description: "Reorder, move and resize.") {
    state side = 240
    state tabs = ["App.wf", "Home.wf"]
    state rows = ["src", "public", "README.md"]
    state landing = ""

    action moveTab(what: String, near: String, position: String) {
        let rest = tabs.filter((x) => x != what)
        let at = rest.indexOf(near)
        let i = if position == "after" { at + 1 } else { at }
        tabs = [...rest.slice(0, i), what, ...rest.slice(i)]
    }

    Heading("Files").h1
    Row {
        Stack {
            style { width: {side}px }
            for r in rows {
                Card(drag: r, dragGroup: "tree", dropValue: r, dropInside: r == "src") {
                    on receive(d) { log("{d.value} goes {d.position} {r}") }
                    on dragmove(d) { landing = if d.over == null { "" } else { "Goes {d.position} {d.over}" } }
                    on longpress(p) { log("menu for {r}") }
                    Text(r)
                }
            }
            Text(landing)
        }
        Splitter(bind: side, min: 160, max: 480, reset: 240, label: "Sidebar width")
        Row {
            for tab in tabs {
                Card(drag: tab, dragGroup: "tabs", dropValue: tab, dropAxis: .x) {
                    on receive(d) { moveTab(d.value, tab, d.position) }
                    Text(tab)
                }
            }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`drag: value`** picks an element up: a mouse moved a few pixels, or a finger held still for half a second. A ghost follows the pointer, a box the pointer passes near the edge of scrolls, and Escape puts the element back. The click a drag ends with is not a click.\n- **`on receive(d)`** takes a drop. `d.value` is what was dropped, and `d.position` is where:\n  - `\"before\"` or `\"after\"` along `dropAxis:` — `.y`, down the page, is the default; `.x`, across it, is for a tab strip;\n  - `\"inside\"`, on the middle of a target with `dropInside: true` (a folder, a container).\n  - The target shows a line where the drop would go, or an outline for inside.\n- **`on dragmove(d)`** on the dragged element hears, as it moves, what it is over — the target's `dropValue:` as `d.over`, null over nothing — and where it would land. That is enough to say \"Goes after Badge, inside Row\".\n- **`dragGroup:`** on a drag and its targets keeps groups apart, such as tabs and tree rows.\n- **`on longpress(p)`** is half a second held still (`p.x`, `p.y`); the click it ends with is not a click. **`on swipe(s)`** is a finger or a pen swept across: `s.direction` is `\"left\"`, `\"right\"`, `\"up\"` or `\"down\"`.\n- **`Splitter(bind:, min:, max:, reset:)`** sizes the region beside it:\n  - It follows the pointer between its bounds; a double-click or Enter resets it; the arrow keys move it 16 px (64 with Shift).\n  - `.horizontal` sits between regions one above the other; `.reverse` sits on the start side of what it sizes — a pane on the right, a panel at the bottom.\n  - A drag or a key starts from the region's size as it is drawn. So the bound value may mean \"the default share\" — `0`, with the region's width a percentage until it is dragged — and `reset: 0` goes back to it: a reset is taken as written, not held to `min:`.\n  - It is a `separator` in the tab order unless its `role:` or `tabindex:` says otherwise.\n\nOn a call of your own component, all of these — the drag, the drop, the gestures — apply to the one element it draws, as a DOM event's handler or an `aria-label` written on the call does:")

        CodeBlock(#"component TreeRow(_ name: String) {
    Row { Icon("file")  Text(name) }
}

page Files(path: "/files", title: "Files", description: "Tree rows you can drag.") {
    state rows = ["src", "README.md"]
    Heading("Files").h1
    Stack {
        for r in rows {
            TreeRow(r, drag: r, dragGroup: "tree", dropValue: r, dropInside: r == "src") {
                on receive(d) { log("{d.value} goes {d.position} {r}") }
                on contextmenu(e) { log("menu for {r}") }
            }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A component that declares a prop or an event of that name takes it itself. A component whose body is an `if`, a `for` or several elements has no one element for them, and the build says so (`C07`): wrap what it draws in a `Stack` or a `Row`.")

        Callout("A drag has no keyboard of its own. Give what it does a menu item or a command too (`Move left`, `Move to…`), as the platform's accessibility rules ask of every pointer action.", kind: .important)

        Heading("Listening outside the page's elements", id: "listening-outside-the-page-s-elements").h2

        Markdown("A handler on an element hears that element. For the window, write the handler at the top of the page, a component or `app`, outside any element — `resize`, `scroll`, `hashchange`, `popstate`, `online`, `offline`, `message`, `storage`, `visibilitychange`, `blur` and `focus` are heard there ([Shortcuts for an app](#shortcuts-for-an-app)). It listens for as long as its owner shows:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Scrolled(path: "/", title: "Scrolled", description: "How far down the reader is.") {
    state y = 0
    on scroll { y = window.scrollY }
    Heading("Scrolled").h1
    Text("{y}px down")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("For any other target or event, use an `effect` with a `cleanup`, which removes the listener when the page leaves. An action named without parentheses is the function itself, so you can hand it to the browser:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Selection(path: "/", title: "Selection", description: "What the reader has selected.") {
    state picked = ""
    action track() { picked = document.getSelection().toString() }
    effect {
        document.addEventListener("selectionchange", track)
        cleanup { document.removeEventListener("selectionchange", track) }
    }
    Heading("Selection").h1
    Text("Selected: {picked}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("For the viewport's size, the network and the URL, the language already keeps values current — `viewport`, `network`, `query`, `hash` — so no listener is needed ([State](/docs/guide/state#the-browser-as-values)).")

        Heading("Summary", id: "summary").h2

        Table(caption: "Summary") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You write").header
                    Table.Cell("It does").header
                    Table.Cell("Section").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on click \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("runs when the element fires the event")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Handlers](#handlers)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on keydown(e) \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("names the event object")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[The event object](#the-event-object)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on key(\"Escape\") \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("answers to one key or combination")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Keys](#keys)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on key(\"mod+k mod+s\") \{ … \}` on `app`") }
                    Table.Cell("an app-wide shortcut, here a chord")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Shortcuts for an app](#shortcuts-for-an-app)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`keyIs(e, \"mod+s\")`, `keyLabel(\"mod+s\")`") }
                    Table.Cell("test a key event; write a combination for this system")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Shortcuts for an app](#shortcuts-for-an-app)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`event change(v: Number)`, `emit change(n)`") }
                    Table.Cell("a component's own event")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Emitting events from components](#emitting-events-from-components)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Modal(visible: open, title: \"…\")`") }
                    Table.Cell("a dialog opened by state")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Overlays](#overlays-modals-dialogs-toasts-and-menus)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Popover(visible:, anchor:)`, `Menu(visible:, at:)`") }
                    Table.Cell("layers placed at a control or a point")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Layers](#layers-popovers-menus-and-tooltips-above-everything)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`drag: v`, `on receive(d)`, `on dragmove(d)`") }
                    Table.Cell("drag and drop")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Gestures](#gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on longpress(p)`, `on swipe(s)`") }
                    Table.Cell("a held press; a sweep")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Gestures](#gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Splitter(bind: side, min:, max:)`") }
                    Table.Cell("a handle that sizes a region")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Gestures](#gestures-drag-long-press-swipe-and-the-splitter)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on scroll \{ … \}` outside any element") }
                    Table.Cell("a window event")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Listening outside the page's elements](#listening-outside-the-page-s-elements)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The [components reference](/docs/reference) lists every event and prop of each built-in.")
    }
}
